    <section class="section">
      <h2 class="shead">How it works</h2>
      <div class="rule-orn"><span>&#10086;</span></div>
      <ol class="how">
        <li>Paste your publication&rsquo;s URL above. The instant specimen uses public archive metadata;
        paid writing enters the concierge beta through Substack&rsquo;s own export, never your password.</li>
        <li>We typeset a print-ready edition and send you the exact proof PDF. You mark corrections;
        we make them and ask for written approval before printing.</li>
        <li>Once approved, the edition is published through Lulu. Lulu prints each copy on demand,
        takes the reader&rsquo;s payment and ships it directly.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 class="shead">In your newsletter</h2>
      <div class="rule-orn"><span>&#10086;</span></div>
      <p>Readers meet the edition where they already read you: one link at the foot of a post,
      or on your About page, goes straight to the printed copy. Buying it is the link, then
      Lulu&rsquo;s checkout. Nothing of ours in between.</p>
      <figure class="newsmock" aria-label="Example of the print link at the end of a Substack post">
        <div class="nm-run"><span class="nm-pub">Caithrin</span><span class="nm-date">Aug 28</span></div>
        <p class="nm-body">&hellip;and that, more than anything, is why an archive deserves paper.
        Thanks for reading.</p>
        <a class="nm-btn" href="https://www.lulu.com/shop/caithrin-rintoul/caithrin-collected-essays-20252026/paperback/product-m2v2e82.html">Get a print copy &mdash; the 2026 annual, at cost</a>
        <div class="nm-foot">Subscribe &middot; Share &middot; Get a print copy</div>
        <figcaption>The live example above is real: it links to the caithrin edition at its exact print cost.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="snippet" id="snippet" hidden>
        <div class="snip-label">Your button. Paste it into any post or your About page:</div>
        <pre class="snip-code" id="snip-code"></pre>
        <button class="snip-copy" id="snip-copy" type="button">Copy the code</button>
        <p class="note">Today the link reproduces your preview for your readers; when your
        edition is live it goes straight to your Lulu page.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 class="shead">Who the beta is for</h2>
      <div class="rule-orn"><span>&#10086;</span></div>
      <p>Substack writers with roughly 1,000 to 20,000 subscribers, at least a year of archive, and
      essays or serialized nonfiction. Every publication in the beta gets concierge treatment: a
      person, not a pipeline, checks every page of your first issue.</p>
      <p>The clearest beta uses are an annual your readers buy at your price, a thank-you
      edition for founding subscribers, and a physical door into the back catalog for new
      readers. Your margin is yours the day payouts ship.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section faq">
      <h2 class="shead">Questions writers ask</h2>
      <div class="rule-orn"><span>&#10086;</span></div>
      <details open>
        <summary>What does it cost me?</summary>
        <p>Nothing. The service is free during the beta. Readers pay the printer for each copy:
        a 294-page book landed at $16.58 on a real order, and thinner books cost less.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What do I earn?</summary>
        <p>Whatever you choose. List at print cost, or add a margin; the margin is yours. Beta
        editions default to exact cost while payouts get built with beta authors on measured
        numbers, so nothing is promised before it exists.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Who owns the writing?</summary>
        <p>You do. Nothing prints until you approve the proof. Beta permissions and takedown terms
        are confirmed in writing before an edition is listed.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is every book perfect bound?</summary>
        <p>Yes. Every beta edition is a 6×9 perfect-bound paperback. Rather than push one volume to
        the binding&rsquo;s limits, we cap volumes near 300 pages: a longer archive becomes a quarterly
        set of thinner books. Hardcover comes later, if beta publications want it.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How do paid posts get in without my password?</summary>
        <p>You upload the export Substack generates in your own dashboard (Settings &rarr; Exports).
        Any subscriber files inside it are discarded unread; only the posts are kept.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What happens to images, tweets and embeds?</summary>
        <p>The renderer reports missing images and replaces web-only embeds with printed source
        notes. During beta, we inspect that report and the proof with you before printing.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>When do the first books exist?</summary>
        <p>The beta targets December printing, with books in readers&rsquo; hands around New Year. Beta
        publications hear every date change directly.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Where can I read more?</summary>
        <p>Write to <a href="mailto:caithrin@caithrin.com">caithrin@caithrin.com</a>; every message gets
        an answer. The build is documented as it happens on
        <a href="https://www.caithrin.com">caithrin.com</a>.</p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section class="beta" id="beta">
